~ core.io — output to file descriptors.
~ Import with `<< core.io`; the exports are reached through the `io` binding:
~   io.write(content, fd) ~ write content's rendered bytes to a file descriptor (no
~                           newline); returns the number of bytes written (Num)
~   io.print(x)           ~ write x to stdout, with a trailing newline (any renderable value)
~   io.eprint(x)          ~ same, to stderr
~   @readStdin()          ~ read one line from stdin (Text, without the trailing newline);
~                           an `@` primitive keeps its bare name once the module is imported
~   @streamFile(path, chunkSize, onChunk)   ~ read a file in chunkSize-byte chunks, calling
~                                             onChunk(chunk) per chunk; Ok(bytesRead)/NotOk(msg)
~   io.streamFile(path, onChunk)   ~ @streamFile with a runtime-chosen default chunk size
~   io.stdout, io.stderr  ~ the standard file descriptors (Num: 1 and 2)
~
~ Examples:
~   << core.io
~   ^ = () -> Num => <
~     io.print("hello")              ~ prints: hello\n
~     io.print(42)                   ~ prints: 42\n
~     io.write("raw", io.stdout)     ~ prints: raw   (no newline)
~     io.eprint("oops")              ~ to stderr, with a newline
~     0
~   >
~
~ `print`/`eprint`/`write` are compiler-lowered to runtime intrinsics, so the bodies below
~ are inert placeholders — never emitted, and not the place to change behavior.
~ `stdout`/`stderr` are ordinary Num constants (file descriptors).

~ Standard output / error file descriptors.
>> stdout = 1
>> stderr = 2

~ Write a value to stdout followed by a newline. Takes anything renderable: the value is
~ rendered through its type's `` ` `` member. `print(x)` is the ergonomic form of
~ `write(x, stdout)` (plus the newline).
~ Returns Unit (`$`) — the printed value's "result" is meaningless.
>> print = x -> $ => < $ >

~ Write a value to stderr followed by a newline. Takes anything renderable.
~ Returns Unit (`$`).
>> eprint = x -> $ => < $ >

~ Write a value's rendered bytes to a file descriptor (no trailing newline).
~ Returns the number of bytes written. e.g. `write("hi", stdout)`.
>> write = (content, fd :: Num) -> Num => < 0 >

~ Read one line from stdin, returning it as a Text WITHOUT the trailing newline.
~ `@readStdin` is a leaf IO primitive (the `@` marker): calling it launches the read in the
~ background and hands back a DEFERRED Text immediately — the fiber only waits (forces) once
~ a strict operation reads the bytes (a comparison, `print`, a native call, ...). At
~ end-of-input it yields the empty Text `""`. The body below is an inert placeholder.
>> @readStdin = () -> Text => < "" >

~ Read `path` in `chunkSize`-byte reads, calling `onChunk` once per chunk — on the calling
~ fiber, parking when a read reports not ready (a pipe or FIFO; a regular file's reads return
~ at once). Every chunk is whole, valid Text (an incomplete UTF-8 sequence or grapheme cluster
~ at a read's end carries into the next one); `onChunk` returns `true` to keep reading or
~ `false` to stop and close the file at once. Yields `Ok(bytesRead)` (the total bytes
~ delivered) at end-of-input or on a stop, or `NotOk(message)` on any failure (a missing file,
~ a read error, invalid UTF-8 in the file, a `chunkSize` that is zero, negative, or fractional,
~ or a `chunkSize` too large to allocate a chunk buffer for) — never fails the program. The
~ body below is an inert placeholder.
>> @streamFile = (path :: Text, chunkSize :: Num, onChunk :: (Text) -> Bool) -> Result => <
  NotOk("")
>

~ 64 KiB: per-chunk cost is the callback, so a file default is large.
kb64 = 65536

>> streamFile = (path :: Text, onChunk :: (Text) -> Bool) -> Result => <
  @streamFile(path, kb64, onChunk)
>
